SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Matias Strehl-Pessina\, University of California
 \, Santa Barbara - Can Targeted VAT Exemptions Make VAT Less Regressive?
  Evidence from a Welfare-Program Reform in Uruguay
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Matias Strehl-Pessina\, University of California\, S
 anta Barbara\nTitle: "Can Targeted VAT Exemptions Make VAT Less Regressi
 ve? Evidence from a Welfare-Program Reform in Uruguay"\n\nVAT taxes are 
 a major source of public revenue\, especially in Latin America\, but the
 y are often regressive. Traditional VAT exemptions for basic goods are c
 ostly\, poorly targeted\, and may be partly captured by firms through hi
 gher retail prices. This paper studies an alternative policy design: a t
 argeted VAT rebate for low-income households beneficiaries of a welfare 
 program. I ask whether participating retailers responded by increasing p
 osted prices\, thereby capturing part of the intended benefit. Combining
  administrative program records with scanner retail data\, I use a diffe
 rence-in-differences design comparing stores with different pre-reform e
 xposure to program-card sales. I find that posted prices increased by ab
 out 3 to 4 percent only in stores highly dependent on program sales\, wh
 ich represent a small share of total program purchases. For most program
  purchases\, prices did not increase. The results suggest that targeted 
 VAT exemptions can reduce VAT regressivity while avoiding fiscal spendin
 g on higher-income households and minimizing retailer capture\, though m
 arket conditions remain important for incidence. JEL Clasification Codes
 : H21\, H22\, H23\, I38\, L81\, O12\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/
 2026/practice-job-talk-matias-strehl-pessina-university-california-santa
 -barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-matias-strehl
 -pessina-university-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
