UID:4525f480e09577d97162@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20260930T181710Z
DTSTART:20261020T223000Z
DTEND:20261021T000000Z
SUMMARY:Graduate Events: Donovan Rasamoelison\, University of California\
 , Santa Barbara
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Donovan Rasamoelison\, University of California\, Sa
 nta Barbara\n\nThis paper investigates whether compliance documentation 
 offers incremental fraud deterrence in the presence of initial ex-ante s
 creening in public programs by leveraging a policy feature of the Payche
 ck Protection Program - a loan-size threshold that waived compliance doc
 umentation requirements for loan forgiveness. On one hand\, the waiver m
 ay encourage firms with true loan eligibility below the threshold to opp
 ortunistically inflate their loan requests up to the threshold. On the o
 ther hand\, firms with true loan amounts above the threshold may strateg
 ically reduce their loans to avoid the administrative burden of complian
 ce. Quantitatively\, opportunistic overclaiming was limited and the loan
 -reducing effect outweighed the loan-increasing effect\, resulting in a 
 net welfare gain of $0.03 per dollar borrowed from the documentation wai
 ver. These results indicate that compliance documentation requirements i
 mpose substantial burdens while providing limited marginal fraud deterre
 nce in the presence of initial screening.\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/event
 s/all/2026/practice-job-talk-donovan-rasamoelison-university-california-
 santa-barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-donovan-rasam
 oelison-university-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:GRADUATE
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
